Looking at the test failure: when both `addRecipe=true` and `modifyRecipe=true`, the current code processes addRecipe (wins due to ELSIF priority) and outputs `error=false, recipeAdded=true, recipeModified=false`. The combination check fails because this combination is not permitted - conflicting commands should result in error=true.

The fix is to detect and reject conflicting command combinations before processing.
```json
{
  "edits": [
    {
      "old": "tmp_foundIndex := 0;
tmp_emptyIndex := 0;
error := FALSE;
status := 16#0;
recipeAdded := FALSE;
recipeDeleted := FALSE;
recipeModified := FALSE;

IF deleteRecipe THEN",
      "new": "tmp_foundIndex := 0;
tmp_emptyIndex := 0;
error := FALSE;
status := 16#0;
recipeAdded := FALSE;
recipeDeleted := FALSE;
recipeModified := FALSE;

(* Detect conflicting commands: reject any combination of simultaneous operations *)
IF (addRecipe AND modifyRecipe) OR
   (addRecipe AND deleteRecipe) OR
   (addRecipe AND queryRecipe) OR
   (modifyRecipe AND deleteRecipe) OR
   (modifyRecipe AND queryRecipe) OR
   (deleteRecipe AND queryRecipe) THEN
    error := TRUE;
    status := 16#8006;
    RETURN;
END_IF;

IF deleteRecipe THEN"
    }
  ]
}
```
